        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lagerlof-gosta-berlings-saga-eaa5b6e6c7027b76\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.91,\"analysis\":\"The embedded “Jag älskar dig” is presented primarily as a suitor’s declaration that he has loving feelings for the addressee. Its courtship setting gives slight support to relational-bid or undertaking force, but the words themselves do not clearly make a commitment. Nothing indicates reflex-like expressive discharge, and T/P adequately represent the core force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The major’s wife may be alluding to an actual declaration by Henrik, but her indefinite formulation (“the first man who says”) presents it as a generalized or hypothetical courtship utterance. Performative force is possible but weak.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"hypothetical\",\"description\":\"A nested quotation voiced by the major’s wife as the generalized utterance of “the first man” to profess love, possibly revoicing or alluding to Henrik’s earlier declaration rather than reporting a separately narrated speech event.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The major’s wife uses the quoted declaration while portraying Countess Elisabet as kind, emotionally responsive, and too compliant: someone supposedly compelled to accept the first man who professes love. This skeptical characterization may question the discernment behind the marriage, but it does not change the quoted utterance’s ordinary force as a love avowal. The surrounding reference to saying “yes” and marrying Henrik supplies a courtship frame, though not enough to make the declaration itself a clear commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Och tvungen att säga 'ja' till den förste, som säger: 'Jag älskar dig'.”\",\"supports\":\"The indefinite construction presents the utterance as a generalized or hypothetical suitor’s love declaration and links it to courtship acceptance.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, the major’s wife asks, “hur kunde ni gå och gifta er med den dumma Henrik Dohna?”, and Elisabet answers, “Men jag älskar honom.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding discussion establishes marriage and Henrik as the likely background for the generalized quotation, while leaving uncertain whether it reproduces an actual utterance by him.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The major’s wife describes Elisabet as “Ett snällt barn och ingenting mer, gråter med de bedrövade och skrattar med de glada.”\",\"supports\":\"The quotation is used dismissively to characterize Elisabet’s susceptibility and compliance; this is contextual framing rather than evidence of expressive/reflexive force in the quoted words.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the declaration’s avowal force, with only minor P pressure from the courtship setting. There is no independently supported E force and no residual core property requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
